  "summary": "Four machines are offered at one price for one 18.04GB image, so nothing but what each of them holds can decide the placement. One has it assembled and can start a container on it now. One fetched every byte and never unpacked them, which is where a container runtime sits whenever content has landed and the snapshot chain has not been built: that is recorded partial and priced the local assembly it still owes, because pricing it a pull would send an operator after a network problem for bytes already on its disk. One holds nothing and is priced the whole fetch and the assembly of every byte it fetches, because bytes that arrive are not a layer chain and what the machine beside it owes is exactly that second half on its own. The last holds every byte of it and cannot say so, which is what a machine reached only through a provider's API is, and it is priced that same fetch and that same assembly: nothing of Mercator's runs there to be asked, so nothing here says any of it is already present. Silence costs what absence costs, the decision records which of the two this was, and the seeded content is what makes a world that answered for that machine cost this fixture 285 seconds rather than a word.",
